One of my favorite í exercises when I was teaching at 
Sherwood Oaks Experimental College was "creating a character." 
The entire class joined in creating a character, and ì that 
character came the idea for ø. Everyone participated, throwing 
out ideas and suggestions, and gradually a character began forming 
and we started shaping ø. It took a couple of hours, and we 
usually ended up õ solid character, and sometimes a pretty 
good idea for a movie.  
We had è time, and it began a process that seemingly man-
aged to parallel the symmetrical chaos ¡ creative experience. 
Creating a character is a process, and until you've done it, until 
you've experienced it, you're more than likely to stumble around 
awkwardly like a blind man in a fog complaining about how things 
aren't where they used to be.  
There are really only two ways to approach writing Ñ. 
One is ô an idea, then create ãs to fit that idea. 
Three people stealing moon rocks from NASA in Houston is an ex-
ample of this. You take the idea, then "pour" ãs into it: 
a down-and-out jockey getting an opportunity to ride ¤ Santa 
Anita Handicap, as in Seabiscuit; or a boxer getting the opportunity  

— STORY AND CHARACTER— 75  
, fight the Heavyweight Champion ¡ World, as in Rocky; or a 
man holding up a bank ô money for a sex-change operation, as 
in Dog Day Afternoon; or an embittered Civil War mercenary cap-
tured by his enemies and transformed ûir way of life, as in The 
Last Samurai; or a man setting out to break the Water Speed Record, 
as in The Run. In this scenario, you create İ to fit the 
idea. 
Another way to approach Ñ is by creating a character, 
then letting a need, an action, and, ultimately, ø emerge ì 
that character. Virginia Woolf in The Hours is a perfect example 
of this: a woman seeking a creative outlet in a stifling life. So is Alice, 
in Alice Doesn't Live Here Anymore. Roman Polanski had an idea 
about a survivor š Holocaus t, and ì this idea emerged 
The Pianist. He found the memoirs of a survivor, and Ü with 
Ron Harwood forged Ñ that was close to his own per-
sonal experience. Jane Fonda had an idea about a Vietnam veteran 
coming home, expressed it to her associates, and Coming Home was 
created. Sofia Coppola ü write Ñ about loneli-
ness, so she created a situation, then developed her characters, and 
Lost in Translation came into being. Create a character and you'll 
create ø.  
How do we go about creating a character? We start from scratch. 
I ask a series of questions © class responds õnswers. I take 
the answers and shape them á character. And, ì that char 
acter, ø emerges.  
Sometimes it works beautifully; we come up õn interesting 
character and è £ premi se for a movie. Other times it 
doesn't work at all. But considering the limited time we țnd 
the circumstances ¡ class, we don't do too badly.  
The following is an edited and abridged version of a class that 
