Seabiscuit 
Gary Ross  
The story of Seabiscuit is a unique and inspirational one, not only 
for ř horse accomplished but Ó way in which he ac-
complished it. During the second half ¡ 1930s, Seabiscuit be-
came a cultural icon throughout the length and breath ¡ land. 
He was universally praised, his exploits and appeal so legendary that 
the very name "Seabiscuit" seemed to transcend the sport itself.  
But this was more than just ø of a physically and spiritually 
broken horse. With his extraordinary rise to fame, he became a 
symbol of hope; here was ø of three men and a horse striving 
for some Ö belief and faith to hold on to ¤ midst of 
the Great Depression. Seabiscuit became the object ¡ nation's 
faith, someone to root for, something we could believe in. As a 
