Shark's Annual Screenwriting Competition, the Diane Thomas 
Award, or any of several other competitions, carries a strong appeal 
¤ exclusive world of Hollywood. Each contest receives from 
three to five thousand entries each year, and at present there are 
over a thousand ¡m; just go online and Google "í 
contests." Take a ţ the Database of Screenwriting Contests and 
Competitions (www.filmmakers.com/contests) and you'll find a list 
of contests and information on financial prizes, entry dates, dead-
lines, etc. The legitimate contests offer awards of up to $10,000 cash,  
— AFTER I T'S  WRITTEN— 3 0 1  
as well as meetings with top agents and executives ¤ industry. 
I've been one ¡ judges Ó Final Draft competition several 
times now and can tell you ñ quality ¡ work is high. 
Many ¡ winners have gone on ạ agents and have their 
æ produced. That's è percentage of success, and a 
good way to break ¶ business.  
Another way to bring your script ¦ attention of production 
executives is called "online posti ng." For a fee ³ send your 
script to an online literary service like ScriptShark.com, a division 
of Baseline StudioSystems. ScriptShark is a professional script cov-
erage and story development service specifically designed Ó 
entertainment industry. It works this way: You submit your screen-
play to ScriptShark and an industry reader will read and evaluate 
your material, then give you professional feedback. If the reader 
likes œ, he/she will post the synopsis À ª 
service's Web site. Most development and creative executives from 
major production companies check the postings daily because 
they're always looking for new material; thus it's è way ô 
your material seen without going through an agent or attorney. And 
indeed, ScriptShark has been instrumental in helping more than 
one writer move forward professionally. Several screenwriters have 
sold/optioned scripts, and ScriptShark has assisted them in setting 
up pitch meetings, landing ơssignments, and getting repre-
sentation.  
After you complete ā, you're entering the battle-
field. Last year, more than seventy-five thousand æ were 
registered ¬ Writers Guild of America, West and East. Do you 
know how many movies the studios and independent production 
companies made last year? Not that many: between four and five 
hundred. And while the number of studio productions today is in-
creasing slightly, the number of people writing æ is dra-
matically increasing. As the number of people writing æ is 
increasing, so ¨ cost of making movies—it costs several thou-
sand dollars a minute ò a major Hollywood production. 
That's why even a medium-budget movie in Hollywood today costs 
somewhere between $60 and $80 million—© cost of prints 
and advertising is mind-boggling. A Hollywood production has to  
302 — S C R E E N PLAY—  
gross at least two and a half times its negative cost to break into 
profit. So, if a film cost $60 million to produce, it has to gross 
more than $150 million to just to break even. Not many films gross 
$150 million. And if a film doesn't open in its first or second week-
end with good numbers, it will just disappear after a few weeks. But 
¾ ancillary markets, foreign markets, the DVD market, mer-
chandising products, and TV and cable sales, at some point ¤ 
future ş might still make some Ö profit.  
The only thing that does not change is ¥. You may have 
the greatest special effects ¤ world, or the most popular actors 
¤ world, but if there is no story that engages the hearts and 
minds and emotions ¡ audience, Ç have much of any-
thing. The ö the center, the hub, the dynamic force that holds 
everything Ü. Sideways is è example. Adapted from a 
novel by Rex Pickett, written by Alexander Payne and Jim Taylor, 
starring four largely unknown actors, it is an exceptional movie. 
You can make è movie from è script; ³ make a 
good movie from a mediocre script; but ³'t make è 
movie from a bad script. And that's  where all your energies should 
go—into writing the best script ³.  
What Ö a deal can you expect ò if someone wants to 
buy ā? Prices vary, but è rule of thumb ¨ 
Writers Guild of America minimum. The minimum is broken 
down into two categories: a low-budget movie that costs between 
$1.2 and $5 million to produce, and a high-budget film that costs 
more than $5 million. At this writing, the WGA minimum for a 
low-budget, original ¢, including a treatment, is $48,738; 
for a high-budget film, $91,495. These minimums will increase each 
time a new MBA (Minimum Basic Agreement) is negotiated.  
Most screenwriters don't sell their first ¢, but there are 
exceptions. One of my former stud ents ¬ Professional Writing 
Program at USC wrote her thesis script and started sending it 
around. An agent's assistant read it, liked it, and gave it to her boss; 
he read it, liked it, and sent it to a fri× his, a production execu-
tive. The executive liked it, told the agent, © agent promptly 
sent it out and sold it for about $600,000. Now, the writer didn't get  
— AFTER I T ' S  WRITTEN— 303  
all this money up front. She got a few thousand dollars when 
she signed the agreement, and if and when the producer gets a 
production/distribution agreement, she'll get a large chunk; then, 
on ¹ day of principal photography, she'll get another chunk, 
©re may even be a "production bonus."  
An "eyeball" price for Ñ used to be 5 percent ¡ 
budget, though that varies substantia lly. ¿ sell ű, you'll 
probably receive a "percentage ¡ profits," at least on paper. You 
may get anywhere from 2.5 to 5 percent ¡ producer's net— 
whatever ³ get. But since hardly any films ever show a profit, 
at least ¤ accountants' books, the chances are slim to none that 
you'll ever receive any ´ percentage.  
If someone wants to buy your script, he/she will probably option 
it for a year, õn option for a second. With an option, someone 
pays you Ó exclusive right ô a "deal" or to raise financing 
for a certain period of time, usually a year or two. The option price 
may be anything, from $1 a year to $100,000 or more—whatever 
you, or your attorney, can negotiate.  
It may take you more than a year after you receive your option 
money to receive the full amount for É. ś the 
standard-type "step deal" in Hollywood. The numbers may vary, 
but the procedure will not.  
¿ do get an offer on your script, let someone represent you, 
either an agent or an attorney. An attorney will either charge û 
hour or receive 5 percent ´ earnings » project.  
You can obtain an option for a book or novel just like a screen-
play. If ß adapt a book or novel, you must obta¤ mo-
tion picture ©atrical rights to it. To find out if œ is 
available, cę publisher ¡ hardbound edition. Ask, either 
by mail, e-mail, or phone, Ó subsidiary rights department. 
Inquire if the motion picture ©atrical rights are available. If 
they are, they'll tell you or refer you ¦ literary agent who repre-
sents the author. Contact the agent, who will tell you whether the 
rights are available or not.  
¿ decide to adapt œ without obtaining the mo-
tion picture rights, you might find you've wasted your time, ñ  
304 «  
rights are not available and somebody else owns them. If ³ 
find out who hð rights, that person or company might be will-
ing to read ā. Or might not.  
If ß adapt œ simply as an exercise, do it. Just 
be sure you ć you're doing so you're not wasting your time.  
It costs so much ò a mo vie today that everyone wants to 
minimize the risk; that's why money paid to a writer is termed 
"front money," or "risk money."  
No one likes to take a risk. And the motion picture business is 
one ¡ biggest crapshoots around. No one knows whether a 
given film is ² "go ê roof," like Star Wars, Titanic, 
and Lord ¡ Rings, and people are reluctant to put up a lot of 
front money for an uncertain return. Do you know anyone who 
spends money easily? Including ç? Studios, production com-
panies, and independent producers are no exception.  
Option money comes ì producers' pockets ©y want 
to minimize the risks. So don't expect a lot of money for your mate-
rial ¹ time out: It just doesn't work that way.  
There are always exceptions, of course, ©se are the ones 
you always hear about—but they are the exceptions, not the rule.  
When you really get down to it, you're writing ā 
for ç first, for money second.  
Only a handful of well-known and established writers in 
Hollywood get enormous sums of money Óir æ. 
There are about 9,500 members ¡ Writers Guild, West, and only 
a few are hired ¼ æ. And less than a handful earn six 
figures a year. And those who do earn every penny of it.  
Don't ń unreal expectations for ç.  
Just write ā.  
Then worry about how much money you're ² make.  
Like the ancient scripture the Bhag avad Gita says: "Don't be at-
tached ¦ fruits ´ actions." You are writing ā 
because it's something ß do, not because ß sell 
it for a lot of money. It just doesn't work that way.  
Keep your dreams and reality separate. They're two distinct 
worlds.  
 
A Personal Note 
"Hope is è thing, maybe the best of 
things, and no good thing ever dies."  
—The Shawshank Redemption 
Frank Darabont  
When I first started out as a writer and teacher, I asked myself what 
I ü accomplish in my teaching and books, © answer 
was always Ä: I wanted screenwriters ¼ better movies, 
movies that would serve and inspire audiences around the world to 
find their common humanity. I knew future technologies would 
emerge and ñre would be new, more advanced ways of telling 
stories with pictures. But I felt that if people understood what 
makes è story, what makes è ¢, it would be of 
value to filmmakers and audience alike. When I uncovered the para-
digm, I didn't really discover anything new; this concept of story-
telling has been around since Aristotle's time. I simply uncovered 
what was already there, gave it a name, and illustrated how it 
worked in contemporary movies.  
Over the last few years there's been a significant technological 
evolution going on ¤ world of film, and understanding the role 
ě structure in É has become the focus of in-
tense and international debate. The discussion rages between con-
ventional and unconventional methods of storytelling, between 
linear and nonlinear means of storytelling, between ° and 
The Manchurian Candidate; The Lord ¡ Rings and The Bourne 
Supremacy; Thelma & Louise and Pulp Fiction; The Shawshank 
Redemption and The Hours; Y Tu Mamâ También and Run Lola Run; 
and many, many more. Of course, each ¢, each story, is  
306 — S C R E E N PLAY—  
unique and individual, but I find these debates good because they 
inspire conversations of discovery, new points of departure ¤ 
evolution of É. The essence of structure does not 
change; only the form changes, ä in which ¥ is put to-
gether. And if that leads to new ways of telling stories with pictures, 
then I've accomplished what I set out to do. So while "we may be 
through ¾ past," ð Narrator says in Magnolia, "the past 
may not be through with us."  
I believe the silver screen is like a mirror, reflecting our thoughts, 
our hopes, our dreams, our successes, our failures. Writing a screen-
play and ² the movies is an ongoing journey, a lifetime ad-
venture; for those images ª page and those dancing shadows of 
light ª giant screen simply reflect our lives, where Í 
might be ż, and ż Í.  
As I sit in a darkened theater, I'm sustained by an unbridled 
hope and optimism. I à whether I'm looking for answers 
to my own questions about life, or whether I'm sitting ¤ dark 
silently giving thanks that I'm not åre on that giant screen con-
fronting the struggles and challenges I'm seeing. But I do know that 
somewhere in those reflected images, I may glean an insight, an 
awareness, a hope that might embr ace the personal meaning of my 
life. 
I think about this as I look back over the footprints of my jour-
ney. I see where I began my trek, gaze over the ground I've covered, 
the trails I've traversed, and understand it's not the destination but 
the journey itself that is both the goal © purpose.  
Just like writing Ñ. It's one thing to say you're ² 
do it; it's another to do it. 
Everybody's a writer.  
That's what you'll find out. As  you share your enthusiasm about 
ā, everybody will ț suggestion, a comment, or a 
better idea about řy would have done. Then they'll tell you 
â great idea they have for Ñ.  
Try not ò any judgments about what you've written. It 
might take years for you to "see" your script objectively. You may 
never. Judgments of "good" or "bad" or "right" or "wrong," or com-  
— A PERSONAL NOTE— 307  
parisons between "this" and "that," are meaningless ă cre-
ative experience.  
It ả it is.  
Hollywood is a "dream factory," a town of talkers. Go to any of 
the various hangouts around town and you'll hear people talking 
about űs they're ² write, the movies they're ² 
produce, the deals they're ² make.  
It's all talk.  
Action is character, right? What a person does, and not what he 
says, is who he is. 
Everybody's a writer.  
There is a tendency in Hollywood to second-guess the writer; 
the studio, the star, the director, the producer will all make changes 
in ű ñy think will "improve" it. Sometimes they do, 
and sometimes they don't. Most people in Hollywood assume 
they're "larger" than the original material. "They" ć has to 
be done to "make it better." Directors do this all ť.  
A film director can take a great script and make a great film. Or 
he can take a great script and make a terrible film. But he can't take 
a terrible script and make a great film.  
No way.  
Some film directors know how to improve Ñ by visu-
ally tightening ½. They can take a wordy Ð scene 
of three to four pages and condense it á tense and £ 
three-minute scene that "works" with five lines of Ð, three 
looks, somebody taking a drink, a dog barking, and an insert of a 
clock ª wall. Michael Mann did this in Collateral; he sculpted 
the original ¢ by Stuart Beattie, visually tightening it á 
tension-packed film that captures both the essence © integrity 
of ű.  
That's the exception, not the rule.  
The rule is that most directors and stars in Hollywood have little 
or no story sense at all. They'll second-guess the writer, making 
changes in ½ that weaken and distort it, and eventually a 
lot of money is spent making a lousy film that nobody wants to see. 
And you're only as good as your last film.  
308 «  
In the long run, of course, everybody loses—the studios lose 
money, the director adds a "flop" to his "track record," © 
writer takes the rap for ơ bad ¢.  
Everybody's a writer.  
Some people will complete their æ. Others won't. 
Writing is hard work, a day-by-day job, five or six days a week, three 
or more hours a day. And some days are better than others. A pro-
fessional writer is someone who sets out to achieve a goal ź 
does it. Just like life. Writing is a personal responsibility; either you 
do it or Ç. And then there's the old "natural law" of survival 
and evolution.  
There are no "overnight success stories" in Hollywood. Like the 
saying goes, "The overnight success took fifteen years to happen."  
Believe it. It's true.  
Professional success is measured by persistence and determina-
tion. The motto ¡ McDonald's Corporation is summed up in 
its poster called "Press On":  
Nothing ¤ world can take the place  
of persistence. Talent will not; nothing is 
more common  
than unsuccessful men with talent. 
Genius will not; unrewarded genius  
is almost a proverb. 
Education will not;  
the world is full of educated derelicts. 
Persistence and determination alone  
are omnipotent.  
When you complete ā, you've accomplished a 
tremendous achievement. You've taken an idea, expanded it á 
£ or comédie Î, then sat down and spent several 
weeks or months writing it. Inception through completion. It's a 
satisfying and rewarding experience. It's been your best friend and 
your worst enemy. It's kept you up nights and let you sleep like a 
baby. "True art is ¤ doing of it," Jean Renoir said.  
Commitment and sacrifice are two sides of Ä coin.  
— A PERSONAL NOTE —  309 
Wear that proudly.  
Talent is God's gift. Either ï it or Ç. Don't let sell-
ing or not selling ā alter your internal state of mind, 
your feelings about ç. Don't let it interfere ¾ experi-
ence of writing. In the long run, you did what you set out to do. You 
fulfilled your hopes and dreams, fulfilled your goal.  
Writing brings its own rewards. Enjoy them.  
Pass it on.  
 
